<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shō Shōken</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1617">1617</a>–<a href="/years/1676">1676</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Haneji Chōshū, Haneji Choshu, Sho Shoken</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shō Shōken, also known as Haneji Chōshū, was a Ryukyuan prince and statesman who served as regent (sessei) of the Ryukyu Kingdom from 1666 to 1673. He cut court and ritual spending, reorganised the administration and promoted agriculture under the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>. In 1650 he completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuzan-seikan">Chūzan Seikan</a>, the kingdom's first official history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shō Shōken was the first son of Prince Chōtai Haneji, third head of Haneji Udun, one of the cadet branches of the royal house. He inherited his father's post of jitō, or district administrator, in 1640. A strong Confucian, he studied under Tonami Jochiku, a pupil of Nanpo Bunshi, and his writings join Confucian views of benevolent rule with the idea of tintoo, the way of heaven, close to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. King Shō Shitsu ordered him to compile the Mirror of Chūzan, the first history of the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>According to the Haneji shioki, his main collection of directives, a royal messenger came in 1666 to offer him the office of sessei. He refused, saying so important an appointment should not be brought by so lowly a messenger, and accepted at once when Inoha Ueekata of the Council of Three made the offer the next day.</p><p>In office he sought to remove whatever in Ryukyuan custom might look backward or undignified to the lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, to whom the kingdom was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. He took the royal family out of many traditional rites, so that they became smaller and cheaper, sidelined the noro priestesses, and punished aristocrats and officials who lived too extravagantly at a time when nobles and peasants alike had fallen into poverty by living beyond their means. Outward Japanese dress and language could not be adopted, since Japanese control had to be hidden to keep good relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He stepped down in 1673.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Mirror of Chūzan tells the kingdom's history through its line of kings, in which poor or malevolent rulers are overthrown by those the Way of Heaven backs. It favours <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>: it presents Ryukyu as Satsuma's loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> long before the invasion of 1609 and blames that invasion on Ryukyuan disloyalty and on the official Tei Dō, known in Okinawan as Jana Ueekata, whom Shō accuses of leading the people astray. It is unclear whether he wrote so from fear of reprisal or from real admiration for Japanese ways, and his wish for Ryukyu to emulate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> calls the accuracy of his history into question.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shō Shōken” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within these limits Ryukyuan statesmen reformed the state: <b>Sho Shoken</b> (Haneji Choshu), regent from 1666 to 1673, cut court expenses and ritual spending, and Sai On, a member of the council of three from 1728, promoted forestry, flood control and agricultural reform.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
